  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $4(1-x)^{6}-1>7.$",
  "worked_solution": "Isolating the sixth-degree term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1-x)^{6}-1 & >7 \\\\ 4(1-x)^{6} & >8 \\\\ (1-x)^{6} & >2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we take the sixth root of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[6]{(1-x)^{6}} & >\\sqrt[6]{2} \\\\ |1-x| & >\\sqrt[6]{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n1-x<-\\sqrt[6]{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,1-x>\\sqrt[6]{2}\n$$\n\nWe can solve each inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-x & <-\\sqrt[6]{2} &  &  & 1-x & >\\sqrt[6]{2} \\\\ -x & <-\\sqrt[6]{2}-1 &  &  & -x & >\\sqrt[6]{2}-1 \\\\ x & >1+\\sqrt[6]{2} &  &  & x & <1-\\sqrt[6]{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is\n\n$$\nx<1-\\sqrt[6]{2}\\,\\text{or}\\,x>1+\\sqrt[6]{2}.\n$$",
